WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:07.280
If we were to encounter alien life, would&nbsp;
we be able to recognize it as life at all?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:13.360
If an organism lived and breathed like animals&nbsp;
on earth, we’d certainly identify it as alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:18.640
But what if some aliens don’t look like organic&nbsp;
life as we know it, or aren’t organic in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:24.480
first place? A project that expands the horizons&nbsp;
of what life on other planets might look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.320
is The Teeming Universe: an interstellar field&nbsp;
guide by speculative author and artist Christian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.840
Cline. This book, which I have a link to down&nbsp;
below, is one I’ve covered a section of before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.120
on this channel. On our previous expedition&nbsp;
through this cosmos, we saw all manner of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.720
incredible species — and learned about the science&nbsp;
behind them. For this entry into the archive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:52.480
we’ll be journeying on a deep space vessel called&nbsp;
‘The Peregrine’ to even more extreme environments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:59.040
We’ll start with planets where the organisms are&nbsp;
truly strange, and as we continue, we’ll reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:06.320
places where we might need a whole new definition&nbsp;
of life… In a far-flung corner of the galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:13.600
our first stop is a planet shrouded in a haze&nbsp;
of dust. This is Aiyte, an extreme world with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:19.520
continent-sized dust storms that keep the surface&nbsp;
in a near-permanent twilight. Stepping out onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.840
the surface, we find a shadow world. Plants&nbsp;
on Aiyte possess black leaves to absorb as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:31.280
much of the sun’s energy as possible. And&nbsp;
animals have gotten even more creative to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.880
adapt to the low light. The first lifeform&nbsp;
we encounter on our voyage is a Helerene — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.480
wolf-sized ambush-predator that stalks the dim&nbsp;
grasslands in search of prey. But what’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:49.200
unusual about the Helerene is that unlike almost&nbsp;
all animals, they possess three eyes — two on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.880
side and a massive one in the middle. This&nbsp;
might seem far-fetched, yet certain species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.320
of real amphibians and reptiles possess tiny&nbsp;
photoreceptive eyes called Parietal, or ‘Solar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:06.000
Eyes’ in the center of their heads. These third&nbsp;
eyes aren’t as complex as their primary ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.120
but are excellent at detecting light. So,&nbsp;
it’s likely that the Helerene’s third eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:17.360
began as solar eyespots, but due to Aiyte’s&nbsp;
dimness became their dominant visual organ.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:24.080
And other animals on Aiyte have followed this&nbsp;
trend. The Sconyx are moose-sized, galloping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.600
herbivores that share the Aiyte’s three-eyed set&nbsp;
up — with two eyes having moved to the side to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.920
keep watch for predators. The Sconyx graze in&nbsp;
large family herds, digging vegetation from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.200
ground with their wedge-shaped beaks and ready&nbsp;
to dart at the first sign of trouble. In many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:48.640
ways these majestic animals are the wild horses&nbsp;
of these plains. A far less majestic lifeform is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.560
the Sandpig. These round, near-sighted animals&nbsp;
spend their time shuffling along the plains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.560 --> 00:03:00.160
foraging for the worm-like creatures they prefer&nbsp;
to feed on. These burrowing animals spend much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:06.400
of their time tunneling snugly underground, only&nbsp;
surfacing in the low-light of dawn to shuffle over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:13.040
and visit the burrows of other Sandpigs. I have&nbsp;
to say, I’m a huge fan of these round creatures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:18.960
As we climb back aboard our spaceship, a trio&nbsp;
of baby Helerenes watch our departure. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:24.720
infants are called pupples, and they’re far less&nbsp;
menacing than their carnivorous parents. They show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:30.800
us that although Aiyte is a planet of dust and&nbsp;
dimness, the beauty of life can still take hold…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:38.240
From orbit, our next planet — Selise — is a&nbsp;
shimmering jewel of the cosmos — an earth-sized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:44.720
temperate paradise with a healthy atmosphere and&nbsp;
glistening seas. Landing on the planet’s surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:50.560
it’s a balmy summer day. Yet despite the seemingly&nbsp;
ideal conditions, there’s not as much terrestrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:56.560
life on this planet as one might expect. Look&nbsp;
to the sky, and the reason becomes clear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.320
Planet Selise has no moon. The lack of a&nbsp;
moon might not seem important to a planet’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:08.480
habitability. But moons help stabilize a planet’s&nbsp;
axial tilt, keeping the weather and temperature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:17.440
more predictable. The push and pull of a moon’s&nbsp;
gravity also help produce intertidal zones:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.560
regions that are essential for life to&nbsp;
transition from water to land. For this reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:29.040
it is probable that most habitable planets in our&nbsp;
universe have at least one orbiting moon. Yet some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:35.680
organisms have hauled themselves onto the surface&nbsp;
of Selise all the same. This is a Santhire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:41.200
one of the planet’s largest land animals and&nbsp;
a testament to the stubbornness of evolution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.240
These odd, segmented creatures still live their&nbsp;
lives tied to the ocean, as they have to lay their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:51.680
eggs in the water like many species of Earth&nbsp;
amphibians. Like frogs, they also have to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.760
their skin wet to aid in respiration, as Santhires&nbsp;
don’t have fully functional terrestrial lungs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:02.800
Their burrows are reflective of their dual&nbsp;
lifestyle, with both aquatic and terrestrial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:09.280
entrances. These nesting grounds show Santhires&nbsp;
are surprisingly skillful homemakers, much like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:14.560
beavers — the semi-aquatic home-building champions&nbsp;
of our own planet. These nesting grounds are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:20.960
vital when the weather shifts. Planet Selise’s&nbsp;
unstable, moonless axial tilt means the climate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:27.520
is highly variable, with some regions frequently&nbsp;
buffeted by cyclones. But not all creatures fear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:34.720
the storm. This small lifeform is called a Behari&nbsp;
Flier, and as its name suggests, it’s one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:40.960
rare lifeforms on Selise that has managed to leave&nbsp;
the water for the air. Due to the intense weather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.080
these creatures have rather extreme patterns&nbsp;
of migrations — moving to the equator in late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:53.120
spring to escape the raging summer winds. To the&nbsp;
south, it’s a beautiful, clear night. This planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.720
is an incredible testimony to life’s persistence,&nbsp;
but it’s time we set off to our next destination…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:05.200
The next stop on our journey promises to be an&nbsp;
unforgettable one. From space, it is clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:11.920
Planet Craelon is the site of massive, artificial&nbsp;
engineering projects. It seems we have at last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:19.440
found advanced alien life… Yet upon the surface,&nbsp;
everything is quiet. Aside from sparse colonies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:26.640
of bacteria on the poles, no biological life&nbsp;
whatsoever is present on this planet. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:34.160
these silent cities are ruled by what we might&nbsp;
term artificial life. A great hivemind of machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:39.280
presides over this planet, which leaves&nbsp;
one to wonder where their makers have gone?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:45.200
Perhaps the creators of this AI left the planet&nbsp;
for better prospects. Or perhaps they died at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:51.680
the hands of their own artificial creations, and&nbsp;
this desolate planet is the site of a mass grave.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:58.720
…Or is it? These machines — called the Crael —&nbsp;
might not fit our traditional standards of life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:03.920
but they are at such a level of advancement and&nbsp;
interconnectivity, they could almost be considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:10.000
an ecosystem. And an ecosystem can be studied.&nbsp;
At the base of this highly unusual environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:16.160
are Scouts. Small, two to three-foot drones,&nbsp;
Scouts appear to be in charge of finding regions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:22.560
in the system to mine for materials to make more&nbsp;
Crael. These mining operations are massive, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:28.080
the machines have almost completely hollowed out&nbsp;
a neighboring moon. The energy needed to complete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:34.880
these operations is possible thanks to another&nbsp;
important lifeform (so to speak). These Crael are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:42.000
Solar Panels, 600-foot-long power collectors which&nbsp;
orbit the sun and transmit vital energy to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:49.280
machines. This division of labor means the Crael&nbsp;
function almost like a bee hive. And the queens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.080
of this colony are the Archships — trapezoidal&nbsp;
monoliths at the top of the Crael hierarcy. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:08:00.720
far the largest type of machine, Arcships are 20&nbsp;
miles, or 32 kilometers in length. Arcships seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:06.560
to function like neurons in a brain, constantly&nbsp;
beaming untranslatable information to other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:13.360
machines and to each other. Indeed, it’s possible&nbsp;
the entire Crael society is better understood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:19.920
as a single, solar-system planet lifeform,&nbsp;
with a single goal… But what is this goal?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.840
Are the Crael mining for their own purposes,&nbsp;
or are they merely following a preset path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:33.040
still active from countless years ago? Fears of&nbsp;
evil robots aside, they don’t seem to be actively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:40.320
malicious. Yet all the same, we might feel safer&nbsp;
once we’ve left this system… But are cold machines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:46.560
the only type of intelligence we’ll find on our&nbsp;
journey? Our next stop is Tevati — and from space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:51.760
we can see lights shining on the surface.&nbsp;
Is this the glow of a kindred civilization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:59.280
or something else? The only way to find out is to&nbsp;
land… Making our descent, we find Tevati is much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:06.160
like other habitable planets we’ve visited… save&nbsp;
for one major difference. Tevati isn’t a planet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:12.720
at all, but a moon orbiting a gas giant similar&nbsp;
to Jupiter. And this moon is considerably smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.840
than Earth, with a fraction of its gravity... And&nbsp;
without the constraints of a strong gravitational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:24.960
pull, life on this moon can become gigantic.&nbsp;
This is a Tevet, a creature which at 30 feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:30.640
or 9 meters tall, wouldn’t be able to stand&nbsp;
up on earth. But they’re perfect for Tevati,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:36.000
where they spend their time grazing on the tops&nbsp;
of tall trees, meaning their lifestyle is similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:42.080
to a giraffe’s. Yet Tevets would tower over a&nbsp;
giraffe of earth, which is why it’s lucky for us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:48.400
these titans are gentle giants. With their&nbsp;
vaguely humanoid body plan, is it possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:54.240
these are our light- makers? It doesn’t seem&nbsp;
so, for while the Tevets aren’t unintelligent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:10:00.720
they’re a long way from civilization building…&nbsp;
Here at last, on the horizon, we can glimpse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:06.480
immense technological structures. These gleaming&nbsp;
wonders are home to a sapient biological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:12.560
species — the first ever encountered apart from&nbsp;
ourselves. Meet the Yaetuans (Yay-tu-ons), a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:18.240
hexapod species that don’t look much like us at&nbsp;
a glance, but have all the necessary ingredients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:24.240
for high intelligence. Possessing both a highly&nbsp;
developed brain and manipulatory appendages with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:30.560
two opposable-thumb like structures, Yaetuans&nbsp;
are kindred spirits in the story of evolution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:37.360
Indeed, they’re quite a bit more advanced&nbsp;
technologically than we are, and luckily for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:43.280
quite friendly. Translating their messages, we&nbsp;
learn their buildings on Tevati are just small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:51.040
colonies. The Yaetuans’ planet of origin is far&nbsp;
more awe-inspiring… With our new friends in tow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:58.000
we’ve arrived at the Yaetuan homeworld — the final&nbsp;
destination of our interstellar expedition. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:04.160
is Yaetu, the gleaming center of a network of&nbsp;
Yaetuan settlements that spans many star-systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:11.120
Landing in the middle of a bustling metropolis, we&nbsp;
can see Yaetuan citizens going about their lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:16.080
Like a colony of ants, Yaetuans are more&nbsp;
communal than humans, and seem to live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:23.280
in remarkable harmony. But it wasn’t always this&nbsp;
way. Like us, Yaetuans have a long and complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:29.040
past — stretching from hunter-gatherers to an&nbsp;
industrial era where the Yaetuans pushed their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:35.600
planet to ecological collapse due to environmental&nbsp;
neglect. It is for this reason that the Yaetuans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:40.000
fled to build colonies on other planets in a mass&nbsp;
exodus, scattering across a dozen solar systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.600
Today, the billions of Yaetuans call different&nbsp;
worlds home, and over thousands of years have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:50.960
become subtly different from one another —&nbsp;
both in language and appearance. Now, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:55.760
their society is reconnecting, as they’ve&nbsp;
restored their homeworld’s environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.760 --> 00:12:01.040
using their advanced technology. While&nbsp;
the modern ecosystem of planet Yaetu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:06.880
is of course dominated by the Yaetus, there are&nbsp;
other notable native lifeforms worth discussing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:13.920
The Wetim are a species of pack hunters, which&nbsp;
have a close relationship to the Yaetuans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:20.080
Like humans and our own canine companions, the&nbsp;
Wetim are the domestic dogs of planet Yaetu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:25.680
having been selected for thousands of years to&nbsp;
be friendly and sociable. Another culturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:31.440
important animal are the Waltef, four winged&nbsp;
fliers that play a vital role in pollinating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:38.560
plant-life on Yaetu — including Yaetuan crops.&nbsp;
As a result, Yaetuans encourage their presence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:44.240
constructing artificial nests on the sides of&nbsp;
buildings to ensure the cities don’t disrupt their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:51.520
habitat… The Yaetuan species in an incredible&nbsp;
story of redemption. In many ways, the Yaetuan’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.520 --> 00:12:57.680
journey from planetary collapse to restoration&nbsp;
is one worth reflecting on. Will humanity repeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:03.520
their mistakes, or learn from their example?&nbsp;
Only time will tell… Meeting and communing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:09.520
the Yaetuans has been a very special experience,&nbsp;
but now the time has come for us to return home…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:16.320
We’ve seen much on our epic voyage through the&nbsp;
stars. Yet Christian Cline’s book The Teeming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:21.120
Universe contains many more incredible&nbsp;
speculative worlds and alien creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:26.880
and I encourage you to check out the link to it&nbsp;
below, and part one of this series. And as always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:32.160
thanks for watching. If you enjoyed this entry,&nbsp;
please lend your support and like, subscribe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:47.840
and hit the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

